  "imageName": "paintpalette.fill",
  "intro": "Russian adjectives must agree with the nouns they describe in gender, number, and case. This means the same adjective will have different endings depending on whether the noun is masculine, feminine, neuter, or plural. In this lesson, we focus on the basic nominative forms - the endings adjectives take when describing the subject.",
  "rules": [
    "Adjectives agree with nouns in gender, number, and case - the ending changes to match.",
    "Masculine adjectives end in -ый, -ий, or -ой: новый дом, синий карандаш, большой стол.",
    "Feminine adjectives end in -ая or -яя: новая книга, синяя ручка.",
    "Neuter adjectives end in -ое or -ее: новое окно, синее море.",
    "Plural adjectives (all genders) end in -ые or -ие: новые дома, синие ручки."
  ],
  "examples": [
    "новый дом (new house), masculine",
    "новая книга (new book), feminine",
    "новое слово (new word), neuter",
    "новые друзья (new friends), plural"
  ],
